# Find-a-Line: append this block to /etc/ufw/after.rules (after its last COMMIT), then
# `sudo ufw reload` (docs/deployment.md, "Firewall"). ufw loads it at boot, before Docker starts;
# Docker never flushes DOCKER-USER, the chain it runs first for traffic forwarded to and from
# containers. Declaring the chain here empties it on every reload, so the rules never pile up.
#
# The containers of Find-a-Line (the bridges br-fal-proxy, br-fal-web and br-fal-worker, named in
# compose.yaml and by `docker network create`) may reach the internet only: no private, link-local
# or carrier-grade NAT address (the LAN, the router, other Docker networks, the cloud metadata
# address, Tailscale), except their own network. Other containers (SWAG on its own network too)
# are not affected. Their DNS goes to public resolvers (compose.yaml: dns), so the router is not
# needed; if CONTAINER_DNS_1 is the router, uncomment the two lines with port 53 and put its
# address there.
*filter
:DOCKER-USER - [0:0]
-A DOCKER-USER -i br-fal-proxy -o br-fal-proxy -j RETURN
-A DOCKER-USER -i br-fal-web -o br-fal-web -j RETURN
-A DOCKER-USER -i br-fal-worker -o br-fal-worker -j RETURN
#-A DOCKER-USER -i br-fal-+ -d 192.168.1.1/32 -p udp --dport 53 -j RETURN
#-A DOCKER-USER -i br-fal-+ -d 192.168.1.1/32 -p tcp --dport 53 -j RETURN
-A DOCKER-USER -i br-fal-+ -d 10.0.0.0/8 -j DROP
-A DOCKER-USER -i br-fal-+ -d 172.16.0.0/12 -j DROP
-A DOCKER-USER -i br-fal-+ -d 192.168.0.0/16 -j DROP
-A DOCKER-USER -i br-fal-+ -d 169.254.0.0/16 -j DROP
-A DOCKER-USER -i br-fal-+ -d 100.64.0.0/10 -j DROP
-A DOCKER-USER -j RETURN
COMMIT
